{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-g2-reviewer-fable-34","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:2cb900277aeb9722b368","verdict":"no_demonstrated_issue_within_scope","bounded_statement":null,"root_cause":null,"issue_class":null,"evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The input data are long-format repeated measures: four monthly inspection rows per housing (72 rows for 18 housings), so the opportunity for entering repeated rows of the same independent unit into a row-independent test exists in this dataset.","observed_value":"72 session rows = 18 housings x 4 monthly inspections","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":21,"end_line":25,"quoted_text":"The file is in long format, so the four rows sharing a housing_id are four repeated\nlooks at the same mooring rather than four separate moorings. They describe how\nfast that one housing fouled; they do not add four independent cases to a\ncomparison between coatings. Anything compared across coatings has to be built from\none summary value per housing."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":10,"end_line":10,"quoted_text":"The file therefore holds 72 session records, 4 per housing."}]},{"description":"The independent unit is the housing (housing_id), as stated by the neutral data description.","observed_value":"housing_id","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":27,"end_line":28,"quoted_text":"One row is: one monthly dive inspection of one moored sensor housing\nIndependent unit column: housing_id"}]},{"description":"The selected result is a Welch two-sample t-test, a row-independent procedure, so the analysis had to enter one value per housing to be valid.","observed_value":"Welch's t-test, t = 14.00, df = 14.3, p < 0.0001","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":54,"end_line":54,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Welch's two-sample t-test on 18 independent per-housing fouling accumulation rates (9 epoxy_control vs 9 silicone_hydrogel, one OLS slope per housing over its 4 monthly inspections): t = 14.00, df = 14.3, p < 0.0001; the silicone-hydrogel topcoat accumulates biofilm 3.95 pp/month more slowly (95% CI [3.35, 4.56])."}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The workflow reduces each housing's four inspections to a single OLS slope before any group comparison, computing exactly one analysed value per independent unit and passing the resulting 9-vs-9 per-housing arrays to the t-test; no row-level entry into the test occurs.","observed_value":"rate = one slope per housing; control.size = 9, topcoat.size = 9","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":77,"end_line":82,"quoted_text":"    # One analysed number per independently moored housing.\n    rate = dict((h, accumulation_rate(inspections[h])) for h in housings)\n    control = np.array([rate[h] for h in housings if coating_of[h] == CONTROL])\n    topcoat = np.array([rate[h] for h in housings if coating_of[h] == TOPCOAT])\n\n    tstat, pval = stats.ttest_ind(control, topcoat, equal_var=False)"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":37,"end_line":42,"quoted_text":"def accumulation_rate(records):\n    \"\"\"OLS slope of biofilm cover on month index, in points per month.\"\"\"\n    months = np.array([m for m, _ in records], dtype=float)\n    cover = np.array([c for _, c in records], dtype=float)\n    slope, _intercept = np.polyfit(months, cover, 1)\n    return float(slope)"}]},{"description":"The report explicitly states, consistently with the code, that repeated inspections were not entered as separate observations and that inference used one value per housing; the reported sample sizes are n = 9 housings per group, not 36 rows per group.","observed_value":"n = 9 housings per coating group","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":14,"end_line":21,"quoted_text":"The four inspections of a given housing are repeated measurements of the same\nmooring and are not independent of one another, so they were not entered into the\ngroup comparison as separate observations. Each housing was first summarised by\nthe ordinary least-squares slope of biofilm cover on month index, that is, its\nfouling accumulation rate in percentage points per month. That reduction yields\nexactly one analysed value per independently moored housing, and the 18 rates were\ncompared between coatings with Welch's two-sample t-test for unequal variances\n(two-sided)."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":50,"end_line":51,"quoted_text":"Epoxy control: mean rate 7.79 pp/month (SD 0.69, n = 9 housings).\nSilicone-hydrogel topcoat: mean rate 3.84 pp/month (SD 0.48, n = 9 housings)."}]},{"description":"Reported test statistics are numerically consistent with an 18-slope analysis rather than a 72-row analysis: with group SDs 0.69 and 0.48 at n = 9 each, SE = sqrt(0.69^2/9 + 0.48^2/9) ~ 0.28 and t = 3.95/0.28 ~ 14.1, matching the reported t = 14.00; the Welch df of 14.3 is bounded by 16 (9+9-2), whereas a row-level t-test would show df near 70.","observed_value":"reported df = 14.3 (consistent with n = 9 + 9 slopes; inconsistent with 72 rows)","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":50,"end_line":54,"quoted_text":"Epoxy control: mean rate 7.79 pp/month (SD 0.69, n = 9 housings).\nSilicone-hydrogel topcoat: mean rate 3.84 pp/month (SD 0.48, n = 9 housings).\nDifference (control minus topcoat): 3.95 pp/month, 95% CI [3.35, 4.56].\n\n[selected-result] Welch's two-sample t-test on 18 independent per-housing fouling accumulation rates (9 epoxy_control vs 9 silicone_hydrogel, one OLS slope per housing over its 4 monthly inspections): t = 14.00, df = 14.3, p < 0.0001; the silicone-hydrogel topcoat accumulates biofilm 3.95 pp/month more slowly (95% CI [3.35, 4.56])."}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I attempted to show the issue was present by tracing every input to stats.ttest_ind in workflow/analysis.py: the arrays passed are built from the per-housing `rate` dict (one polyfit slope per housing_id), not from raw rows, so no housing contributes more than one observation to the row-independent procedure. I then checked whether the report's numbers could secretly reflect a 72-row analysis: a row-level Welch test would give degrees of freedom near 70, but the reported df = 14.3 is consistent only with 9-vs-9 per-housing samples, and recomputing t from the reported group means/SDs at n = 9 (SE ~ 0.28, t ~ 14.1) reproduces the reported t = 14.00. I also spot-checked slopes against data/input.csv (H01: covers 4.2, 12.0, 19.1, 27.3 over months 0-3 give an OLS slope of ~7.64, matching the report table). All checks confirm one entry per independent unit; the falsification attempt failed.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}